From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: natemu <natemu@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] FEXCore: slice1 LOCK forms: one atomic per instruction

Under --profile slice1 (I3):
- LOCK NEG m (AtomicFetchNeg) is one compare-and-swap attempt instead of
  FEX's CAS loop (a native backward branch): the JIT calls the shim's
  FexcLockNeg (a load, one casal, and on a lost race a literal exit to the
  instruction's own RIP). The dispatcher completes the flags and puts CF in
  the exit ABI's form before it, so that exit holds the state before the
  instruction.
- LOCK CMPXCHG / CMPXCHG8B / CMPXCHG16B (recorded in fexc_lock_cas by the
  dispatcher) get `dmb ish` immediately before their casal/caspal: x86 orders
  a locked compare that fails, while a failing Arm CAS writes nothing and has
  only acquire semantics.
- Register allocation never prefers a static register for the result of a
  swap or fetch-and-op atomic (FexcPrecision::LoadsInto, as for a load: the
  destination is UNKNOWN after an abort), and frees the register of one whose
  result is unused (LOCK NOT), as for an unused load.
- A LOCK instruction neither reuses nor leaves a pooled constant: its atomic
  takes the value in a register, and a block-wide pooled constant would hold
  a register from first to last use (slice1 admits no spill).
